# Kit Issues Log

A single, shared record of real bugs, gaps, friction, or **reusable
ideas the kit doesn't have yet** — found in **this kit** (not in
{{PROJECT_NAME}} itself) while using it on this project — kept as one
file everyone appends to, unlike the per-person
`docs/collaborators/<username>.md` logs, because there's usually only a
handful of these a year, not enough for lock contention on concurrent
PRs to be a real concern the way it is for daily work logs.

## The rule this file exists to support

**Finding a bug — or a genuinely reusable idea — in this kit while
working on {{PROJECT_NAME}} is not, by itself, a reason to go open a PR
against the kit repo.** Record it here instead and keep working on
{{PROJECT_NAME}}. See
`.github/AGENT_RULES.md`'s "Finding a bug in the governance kit itself" section for
the full rule and why it changed from this kit's earlier default
(immediately propagating every fix upstream) — in short: fixing the kit
is now treated as its own deliberate, separate unit of work, not
something that happens as an incidental side effect of unrelated project
work, however good the fix.

**The one exception** — an immediate, real-time fix landed directly in
the kit repo, right now, without waiting for a separate session — is only
ever the kit maintainer's own call to make, and only when they
explicitly say so in the moment. It is never something an agent decides
on its own that the situation "clearly" calls for, even in a session the
maintainer is otherwise directing. If that exception is used, still add
an entry here noting it, so this file stays a complete record either way.

**Mechanically true for anyone who isn't the kit's maintainer:** you
almost certainly don't have push access to the kit's own repository, so
the "record here, don't fix there" default isn't really optional for
you — attempting the fix yourself would simply fail at the push step.
That's a real, if incidental, backstop; it doesn't apply to the
maintainer's own sessions, where nothing mechanical stops a same-session
fix, which is exactly why the rule above has to be a stated policy, not
just an access-control side effect.

## How an entry reaches the kit's maintainer

This file living in {{PROJECT_NAME}}'s own repo doesn't, by itself, put
its contents in front of the kit's maintainer — closing that gap is a
real, separate step, not automatic just because the file exists. Two
ways to do it, pick (or combine) what fits:

- **Manual (the default, no setup required):** periodically share this
  file's new entries with the kit's maintainer directly — however you'd
  share anything else (a message, a copy-paste, pointing them at this
  file in this repo if they have access to it). This is what this kit
  assumes happens unless you've deliberately set up the alternative
  below. `.github/AGENT_RULES.md`'s rule requires whatever agent adds an
  entry here to also say so out loud in that same conversation — so the
  human doesn't have to remember to check this file for new entries
  nobody mentioned.
- **Automated, opt-in (real setup required, not wired anywhere by
  default):** `templates/workflows/kit-issues-notify.yml.template` posts
  a cross-repo notification (a link, not this file's contents — see that
  template's own header for why it deliberately doesn't copy entry text
  across a possible public/private trust boundary) to the kit repo when
  this file changes. Requires creating and storing a scoped Personal
  Access Token as a real, deliberate setup step — read that template's
  header in full before wiring it in; it is not a drop-in default for
  the reasons explained there. **GitHub-to-GitHub only** (re-implement
  the intent yourself on another host — see `SETUP-GUIDE.md`'s "A
  different VCS host" section), and **wiring it up is not the same as
  confirming it's actually reaching anyone** — a rotted token or a
  disabled Actions run degrades to a warning line in this repo's own CI
  log, which the maintainer will never see on their own. Treat the
  manual path as the real fallback even after setting this up, not just
  before.

## Entry format

**Read this note before running any setup-time find-and-replace on this
file, including for this project's own name above.** Unlike this file's
one real one-time setup value (the project's own name, filled in near
the top of this file), the double-brace markers in the template block
below are filled in fresh *each time a new entry is added*, by whoever's
adding it — they are not part of this kit's usual one-time "search for
double braces and fill each one in at setup" sweep (see `SETUP-GUIDE.md`
step 2), and nothing below this point should be filled in until there's
an actual entry to record. (This note is deliberately written without
using that literal syntax anywhere in this paragraph, so a blind
find-and-replace over it can't quietly turn this warning into
nonsense.)

```
## {{DATE}} — @{{your-github-username}}

**Kit file/mechanism:** {{which file or mechanism — e.g.
"templates/verify-setup.sh.template" or "the Tier 2 approval protocol"}}
**What happened:** {{the real, specific symptom — not "it doesn't work,"
but what you ran, what you expected, what actually happened}}
**Workaround used here (if any):** {{what you actually did to keep
working on this project — leave blank if you just worked around it
by not using that part of the kit this time}}
**Resolved here at runtime?** {{No. / or "Yes, by @maintainer's
username, see kit PR #NN" if the one real-time exception above was used}}
```

**For the last field specifically, write the literal, bare `No.`**
(nothing after it) when this entry is what it almost always will be —
not resolved at runtime — rather than any other wording that means the
same thing. This one field's exact text matters in a way the others
don't: `verify-setup.sh.template`'s own automated check for unresolved
entries looks for that literal string, and a differently-worded "not
resolved" (`No (not yet)`, `Not resolved`, `No, still open`) won't be
counted — found the hard way, 2026-09-24: an earlier draft of this
template showed `No (default) — or "Yes, by..."` as the example text
itself (not a `{{...}}` placeholder like its neighbors), which broke the
check in both directions at once — real entries had no fixed wording to
copy, and the example line's own text was close enough to a real
unresolved entry that a naive check would have matched the template's
permanent reference block forever, on every downstream copy.

**Recording an idea instead of a defect?** The fields above are
defect-shaped by default, but the same format covers a reusable
improvement — adapt, don't force a fit: use **What happened** for the
idea itself (what it is, why it's better than what the kit does today),
leave **Workaround used here** blank (or record what this project adopted
locally; an idea has nothing to work around), and use **Resolved here at runtime?** the same way (the bare
`No.` unless the maintainer's real-time exception was used to build it
into the kit immediately). **If the maintainer declines an entry**, the
last field should say so with wording that is *not* the bare `No.` (e.g.
`No -- declined upstream (kit vX.Y.Z triage): <reason>`), so
`verify-setup.sh`'s open-entry warning stops counting it.

